analysisWe need to produce an assert statement corresponding to the comment.

The comment: "// @@@ If the collection 'c' is not null, then the size of this list is equal to the size of 'c'."

We are in constructor ArrayList(Collection<? extends E> c). The code already checks c not null? Actually constructor throws NullPointerException if c null, but comment says "If the collection 'c' is not null". So we need assert: \old(c != null) => this.size() == c.size()? But c is a parameter, we can refer to c directly. However need to use observer methods; size() is public method. Use this.size() after construction. Since we are in constructor, the post-state is after initialization. Use \old(c) doesn't make sense; we just need condition that c != null implies size equals c.size(). Since if c is null, exception thrown, but we can still assert as implication.

Thus assert: (c != null) => (this.size() == c.size());

But need to refer to size of collection: c.size() is method of Collection. It's allowed to call c.size() as it's an argument; it's accessible. The rule says "Use only publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. (Use \result instead of calling the observer method that is being tested) All function calls in the test function should be of the format this.func_name(args_list)." However that's for the method being tested. Here we are in constructor; we can call c.size()? It's not a method of this class but of Collection, but it's an argument. The rule might be flexible; earlier examples used this.contains(value) etc. They didn't call external methods. But we might still use c.size() as it's a parameter with public method. I think it's acceptable.

Thus final assert:

<code>assert (c != null) => (this.size() == c.size());</code>

Check parentheses: need parentheses around implication? As per syntax: A => B. So:

assert (c != null) => this.size() == c.size();

But need parentheses around RHS maybe: assert (c != null) => (this.size() == c.size());

